ef 17592186045417] [17592186304618 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c66ea00f-ab02-54a7-8591-f30db0c5b86b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444126 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186444140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186444128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304668 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d7c351da-96ee-57a7-ad19-0d1fa7a32b4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186302693 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186303745 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186304063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186303672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304760 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe78fafd-1fb0-5c2f-9ba1-e3c0b222dbf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466849 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466850 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466851 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-330308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\nI. $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n\nII. ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n\nIII. $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed465271-2eba-5278-8938-505251fa9eb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304814 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because it lacks the particular solution term ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305189 #edb/ref 1080 "unstable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305189 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305189 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0436876d-60a1-53e5-ba1b-6e7feab5be42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305203 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305203 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05cb8cbf-2525-5aa2-adfe-cd481e3a035f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305229 #edb/ref 1080 "images/ef/ef7ed85b18002aeec299826750ecc6306c90db8bf2c9bbd9bd8173af0ee9ea41.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305229 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305229 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "085486de-a0c1-555a-9181-b4b425a4fe03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305283 #edb/ref 1080 "-7\\operatorname{sin}(7t)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305283 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305283 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f24e94e-6875-5dc6-95dc-6ad3d893da7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305285 #edb/ref 1080 "unstable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305285 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305285 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f938149-0198-5c73-a108-bf8eabdfcbd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305289 #edb/ref 1080 "1+6t" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305289 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305289 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0fc7ea02-06bd-5e6c-aed2-4751d80186b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{5}{18}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305343 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921863053